      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we need to use the formula for the distance of the foci from the center of the ellipse.\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is \\(2c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\), with \\(a\\) being the semi-major axis and \\(b\\) being the semi-minor axis. \n\nLet's look at the given diagram. Notice that the largest dimension of the ellipse is along the positive \\(x\\)-direction. Therefore, the semi-major axis \\(a\\) is equal to the distance along the \\(y\\)-axis from \\((0, 0)\\) to the farthest point on the ellipse along the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\nGiven:\n- The length of the semi-major axis \\(a = 6\\).\n- The length of the semi-minor axis \\(b = 2\\).\n\nWe can calculate \\(c\\) as:\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2} = \\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{36 - 4} = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\nSo, the distance between the foci \\(2c\\) is \\(8\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nThe answer is \\( \\boxed{8\\sqrt{2}} \\).",
